<TopicPage slug="subjects/law/expert/comparative-legal-systems">
  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is the capstone that makes the Law track's English legal system comparative. Sources
    of law and What makes a law built the English machinery; How a case moves through court
    and Criminal vs civil law gave you procedure; Contracts, Torts and liability and Property
    law supplied the doctrinal meat. Here every one of those pieces is set beside its
    civil-law, religious-law and mixed counterparts, using the functional method of Zweigert
    and Kötz: compare solutions to the same problem, not surface labels.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>distinguish the common-law, civil-law, religious and mixed families by where each locates law's authority;</li>
    <li>run the functional method: take a problem — dispute rates, lay participation, delegated legislation — and compare each system's answer;</li>
    <li>explain adversarial and judge-directed procedure as two priced solutions to the same accuracy problem;</li>
    <li>trace precedent, purposive interpretation and constitutional review as borrowings that ran in both directions between the families;</li>
    <li>apply the rule-of-law versus rule-by-law gradient to real systems, including the intermediate, contested cases.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Comparative law studies <strong>how different legal systems solve the same human
    problems</strong> — contracts, property, crime, family, procedure. Two great families
    dominate (common law and civil law), with religious and customary systems woven through much
    of the world and mixed jurisdictions proving the borders porous. Comparison is how lawyers
    discover that their own system's features are choices, not necessities. The unit's fifteen
    topics — the anatomy of any legal system — supply the grid: this chapter takes each part of
    the English legal system and sets it beside its civil-law, religious-law and hybrid
    counterparts, using the functional method (Zweigert and Kötz): compare solutions to the same
    problem, not surface labels.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the families, the blends, and the convergence" widget="equationStep" config={compCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the panel compresses the chapter's argument into four
    frames. Steps 1 and 2 present the two great families at their purest — precedent
    accumulating case by case against a code claiming to contain the whole private-law order;
    step 3 adds the systems a two-family story forgets (religious, customary, mixed); and
    step 4 states the empirical result — convergence. Watch where the differences go as you
    move through the steps: they migrate from substance (what the rules say) to procedure,
    legal education and judicial style — which is exactly where the rest of this lesson finds
    them.
  </p>

  <h2>The nature of law across traditions</h2>
  <p>
    Where law's authority comes from differs at the roots. Common law: law is what courts have
    done and will do — Holmes's "prophecies of what the courts will do" — with authority
    accumulating case by case. Civil law (roughly 150 countries, Roman-law roots): law is the
    code — the French Code civil (1804) and the German BGB (in force 1900) claim to contain the
    private-law order, with judges applying rather than making. Religious systems embed the
    question in theology: sharia treats law (fiqh) as the human science of divine obligation,
    so validity's source is God, and state law is a management layer. Radbruch's formula
    (post-1945 Germany) gave natural law its operational comeback: statutes so intolerably
    unjust forfeit legal character — the German courts used it to handle Nazi-era judgments.
    Every family, in short, answers Hart's question differently.
  </p>

  <p><strong>So far:</strong> the families disagree at the root — authority accumulating case by case, codified in advance, or traced to divine obligation. Next comes the comparative scandal: identical rules still produce different behaviour, which is why legal culture, not legal text, is the variable to watch.</p>

  <h2>Law and society, compared</h2>
  <p>
    The comparative scandal is that identical rules produce different behaviour: Japan
    civil-law jurisdiction with litigation rates a fraction of America's — a <em>legal
    culture</em> (Kawashima's explanation: preference for conciliation, social costs of
    confrontation) — while America's common-law cousin England sues far less than either. Alan
    Watson's <em>Legal Transplants</em> argued rules travel easily (the Code civil was exported
    across Europe and Latin America); Pierre Legrand's reply: law is culture, so transplants
    change the words without changing the practice. The evidence lands between: reception
    (Japan's BGB-based code, 1898) created a functioning system whose social meaning remains
    distinct. The functional method's discipline: study the problem (dispute frequency, remedy
    uptake), not the rule's text.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "civil law means law about civil matters." Here "civil law"
    is the family name for the Romano-Germanic codified tradition — the largest in the world —
    not the subject matter. France's <em>droit civil</em> is a whole private-law system;
    confusion of the two senses derails most first comparisons.
  </p>

  <h2>Law and morality: the traditions' answers</h2>
  <p>
    Each tradition draws the law–morality line differently. Codified systems legislate morality
    openly — the Code civil's matrimonial provisions, German family law's "good morals"
    (Sittenwidrigkeit) clause — and let courts apply the codified standard. Religious systems
    refuse the line entirely: in classical sharia, ritual, ethics and law are one curriculum
    (worship, transactions, personal status), so the Wolfenden question — private morality
    beyond law's reach — has no equivalent domain. Common law runs on the Devlin–Hart argument
    made institutionally: morality enters through judicial sympathy, jury verdicts and statute,
    case by case. The comparative lesson: the <em>placement</em> of the line is itself a
    cultural artefact, and constitutional courts worldwide now mediate it (abortion and
    same-sex marriage being the current frontier cases everywhere).
  </p>

  <h2>Justice across systems</h2>
  <p>
    Procedural justice is where citizens feel the difference. Common-law adjudication is
    <em>adversarial</em>: party-controlled investigation, oral evidence tested by
    cross-examination, a neutral judge — justice as fair fight. The civil-law tradition is
    <em>inquisitorial</em> (better: judge-directed): investigating magistrates or panels drive
    evidence-gathering, written dossiers matter, the judge is the active truth-seeker — justice
    as inquiry. Neither is obviously more accurate; the English system prices party resources
    into accuracy, the continental system prices the state's diligence into it. Correction rates
    (reversed convictions) run high enough in both to keep the debate empirical rather than
    ideological. Substantively, the systems converge: damages, restitution and criminal-law
    doctrines map onto each other with functional equivalents almost everywhere.
  </p>

  <h2>Rule of law and rule by law</h2>
  <p>
    The comparative test is whether law binds the state itself. The German <em>Rechtsstaat</em>
    and French <em>état de droit</em> encode the same idea as Dicey's: state action needs legal
    authority, courts review it. The counter-case is rule <em>by</em> law — law as the
    ruler's instrument without binding the ruler: China's system expressly subordinates courts
    to party leadership, and the "rule of law with Chinese characteristics" is the standing
    illustration of Raz's warning that a state can satisfy every formal criterion (published,
    clear, prospective rules) while the law's content serves power. The comparison also
    clarifies the intermediate cases: judiciaries formally independent but episodically
    captured (Poland's court restructuring, 2015–2023, contested with the EU) show the gradient
    is continuous, not binary.
  </p>

  <p><strong>So far:</strong> the deepest test of a system is whether law binds the state itself — and the gradient between rule of law and rule by law is continuous, not binary. The next sections turn from principle to machinery: how each tradition actually drafts, delegates and interprets its rules.</p>

  <h2>How codes and parliaments make law</h2>
  <p>
    Civil-law systems treat legislation as architecture: a code aims to state the whole private
    order in general principles, filled downward by interpretation — the Code civil's five
    books, drafted for a post-revolutionary society, exported wherever French power or
    admiration reached. Common-law systems accumulate: statutes patch, courts systematise — so
    the "law" is a sediment, not a monument. The practical differences are shrinking (English
    legislation now dominates volume) but the drafting cultures remain: continental codes
    generalise and trust courts with abstraction; English statutes particularise and trust
    no one. Mixed jurisdictions show both at once: Louisiana's civil code under a common-law
    shell, Quebec's Code civil in a federal common-law country, South Africa's Roman-Dutch
    common law under parliamentary statute.
  </p>

  <h2>Delegated law-making elsewhere</h2>
  <p>
    Delegation is universal; its constitutional treatment differs. France authorises
    <em>ordonnances</em> (Article 38 of the Constitution: Parliament may delegate to government
    within defined fields, later ratified) — delegation explicit and time-boxed. Germany's
    <em>Rechtsverordnungen</em> require statutory enabling with content limits, policed by the
    Constitutional Court. England's statutory instruments run to thousands a year under
    enabling Acts of varying width. The comparative warning is historical: the German Enabling
    Act of 1933 — delegation without limits, renewed by plebiscite — is the discipline's
    standard case for why enabling clauses need field, duration and judicial review. Every
    modern constitution's delegation rules are drafted in that shadow.
  </p>

  <h2>Interpretation: literal England, teleological Europe</h2>
  <p>
    Interpretive method is the traditions' deepest divergence. English courts began literalist
    (plain meaning, absurdity-corrected), partly as parliamentary-sovereignty discipline; the
    EU years imported the CJEU's teleological method — purpose over grammar — and purposive
    reading survived Brexit in retained law. Civil-law courts were always systematic: interpret
    the provision inside the code's whole scheme (the BGB's general part colours everything);
    and the Code civil's Article 5 forbids judges from issuing general regulatory judgments —
    the codifiers' fear of judge-made law, honoured in form and evaded in practice (the Cour de
    cassation's consistent case law is law in every functional sense). The comparative point:
    methods converge in outcome more than in theory — everyone asks "what did the provision
    mean to do?" eventually.
  </p>

  <h2>Precedent: the common-law export</h2>
  <p>
    Stare decisis — binding past decisions — is the common law's signature technology, and its
    spread is one of comparative law's success stories: even doctrinally precedent-free civil
    systems obey <em>jurisprudence constante</em> (settled line of cases) because predictability
    demands it; the Cour de cassation and Bundesgerichtshof reverse their own precedent rarely;
    the CJEU built a de facto precedent system without a doctrine. Conversely, the
    common-law family codified: American states codify procedure wholesale, and the UK's
    statute book grows annually. The remaining hard difference is craft and form: English
    judgments reason case-by-case at length (the argument IS the judgment); French judgments
    are syllogistic and terse (numbers under the Chapeau). Legal translation between the two
    is a genuine discipline, not a word-mapping exercise.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "civil-law countries have no precedent — every case is
    decided fresh." Formally true, functionally false: settled case lines bind as effectively as
    any doctrine, appeal courts enforce conformity, and commercial life depends on the
    consistency. The doctrine differs; the practice converges — a textbook case of why
    comparative law compares function, not labels.
  </p>

  <h2>Law reform and codification</h2>
  <p>
    Reform machinery reflects family style. Common-law systems: standing commissions (the Law
    Commission, 1965) propose and draft, Parliament enacts — incremental, consultative,
    politically exposed. Civil-law systems: codification is the reform instrument of history —
    the pandectist scholarship behind the BGB took two decades and a scholarly generation;
    France recodifies by commission (the 2016 recodification of contract law by ordinance being
    the recent case). Mixed systems institutionalise both: South Africa's law reform commission
    works across Roman-Dutch and English layers; the Indian codes of the 1860s (contract,
    penal) were the Raj's hybrid — Macaulay drafting English content in civil-law form, and
    they still stand. The transnational layer is new: model laws (UNCITRAL), uniform instruments
    (CISG sales convention, adopted by 90+ states) reform nationally without national
    legislatures drafting.
  </p>

  <h2>The EU and supranational law</h2>
  <p>
    The EU is the world's deepest experiment in law beyond the state — and a civil-law
    supranational court that reshaped a common-law member. Its constitutional doctrines
    (direct effect, Van Gend en Loos 1963; primacy, Costa v ENEL 1964) were CJEU inventions
    from teleological reading of treaties — judge-made constitutionalisation, in a tradition
    nominally hostile to it. While the UK was a member, English courts absorbed the method
    (purposive interpretation, state liability, indirect effect); Brexit retained the body of
    that law as retained (now "assimilated") law while cutting the CJEU's jurisdiction. The
    comparative lesson generalises: supranational courts (EU, ECHR, the East African Court of
    Justice) create hybrid systems wherever they touch national ones — every member state is
    now, in legal-terms, a mixed jurisdiction.
  </p>

  <p><strong>So far:</strong> law-making and interpretation showed the families borrowing in both directions — England purposive, Europe precedent-obedient — and the EU turned every member state into a mixed jurisdiction. Now the comparison reaches the citizen: courts, lay participation, judges and the price of reaching one.</p>

  <h2>Civil courts and procedure, compared</h2>
  <p>
    Civil procedure carries the families' fingerprints. English justice is party-driven with
    active case management (the Woolf reforms, 1998, gave judges control of timetables and costs
    — an inquisitorial import), cost-bearing follows the loser ("loser pays"), and ADR is
    actively pushed by cost sanctions. Continental systems: judge-controlled development,
    fixed statutory fees, loser-pays standard (Germany), and mediation growing unevenly (Italy's
    mandatory mediation for specified claims; the EU's 2008 mediation directive standardising
    encouragement). The distinctive continental institution is the specialised court ladder
    (separate labour, social, tax and administrative courts in Germany and France) against
    England's unified hierarchy with specialist lists. Both solve the same problem — expertise
    in complex disputes — by opposite architecture: specialist courts versus specialist lists.
  </p>

  <h2>Criminal courts, juries and lay judges</h2>
  <p>
    Lay participation in criminal justice takes three institutional forms. The English model:
    lay magistrates disposing of the overwhelming majority of cases, and a 12-member jury for
    serious trials. The French model: the cour d'assises tries the gravest crimes with
    professional judges sitting alongside citizen jurors (reduced in number by the 2019 reform),
    deliberating together — lay voices inside the bench rather than beside it. The German
    model: <em>Schöffen</em> (lay assessors) sitting with professional judges in most criminal
    courts, with equal votes. The American outliers: grand juries (indictment bodies, now mostly
    a prosecutor's instrument) and jury sentencing in some states — both rare worldwide. The
    comparative finding: citizen participation in criminal justice is near-universal in Europe
    in some form; the pure professional trial is the rarer design.
  </p>

  <h2>Judges: career and lateral</h2>
  <p>
    The recruitment divide shapes judiciaries more than any doctrinal difference. Career
    judiciaries (France's École nationale de la magistrature, Germany's judicial track) select
    graduates who enter as trainees and rise through grades — young judges, strong esprit de
    corps, ministry influence, predictable careers. Lateral judiciaries (England, the US) appoint
    mid-career lawyers from practice — older judges, professional independence from the
    executive, more varied backgrounds, but appointment politics where elections apply (US
    states). Mixed systems: Italy's concours-based career judiciary with unusual independence;
    the UK's Judicial Appointments Commission grafting merit panels onto the lateral model.
    Diversity outcomes differ accordingly — career systems have made faster gender progress at
    entry levels; lateral systems eventually recruit from a more varied profession. Neither
    design dominates on quality measures; the choice is a philosophy of what experience a judge
    needs.
  </p>

  <h2>Access to justice across systems</h2>
  <p>
    Each system prices and subsidises justice differently. Cost allocation: the English rule
    (loser pays) deters weak claims but raises risk; the American rule (each side bears its own)
    invites contingency litigation and class actions — the US is nearly alone in mass-tort class
    practice as a private enforcement industry. Legal aid: Germany pairs moderate public funding
    with a functioning legal-expenses insurance market (most households insured); England cut
    eligibility sharply after LASPO 2012; France's aide juridictionnelle is means-tested and
    partial. Delay is the universal tax: Italy's civil dockets run for years, England's courts
    backlog post-pandemic is measured in years for trials — access to justice is now
    quantified in waiting time as much as in fees. The comparative constant: every system rations
    justice somewhere; the families just ration at different gates.
  </p>

  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "the twelve-person jury is the global default for serious
  cases." Lay participation takes radically different institutional forms: Germany's mixed
  courts use professional-plus-lay <em>Schöffen</em> who deliberate as voting equals, France
  uses cour d'assises with magistrates and jurors joined, many civil-law systems reserve
  juries for the gravest charges or none at all. The jury is one answer to "who judges" —
  comparative law is the study of the other answers.
  </p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>A colleague says: "Germany has no precedent — every case is decided fresh." Give the functional correction.</li>
    <li>England prices party resources into accuracy; the continental system prices the state's diligence into it. Name the two procedural designs, and one institutional choice each makes to carry its price.</li>
    <li>Why did the German Enabling Act of 1933 become the standard case for how delegation clauses must be drafted?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) Formally true, functionally false: settled case lines
    (jurisprudence constante) bind as effectively as any doctrine, appeal courts enforce
    conformity, and commercial life depends on that consistency. This is the textbook case of
    why comparative law compares function, not labels — the doctrine differs while the
    practice converges. (2) Adversarial (party-controlled investigation, oral evidence tested
    by cross-examination, a neutral judge) versus judge-directed (investigating magistrates,
    written dossiers, the judge as active truth-seeker). Each carries its price structurally:
    the English design needs resources on both sides — hence loser-pays, cost sanctions and
    pushed ADR; the continental design needs the state's diligence — hence the specialised
    court ladder and fixed statutory fees. (3) Because it was delegation without limits — no
    defined field, no duration, no judicial review, renewed by plebiscite — and it licensed
    the worst law-making in modern history. Every modern constitution's delegation rules
    (France's time-boxed ordonnances, Germany's content-limited Rechtsverordnungen) are
    drafted in its shadow.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Comparative law compares functions, not labels: study how each system solves the same human problem.</li>
    <li>The families locate authority differently — courts (common law), codes (civil law), divine obligation (religious law) — and mixed jurisdictions prove the borders porous.</li>
    <li>Identical rules produce different behaviour; legal culture is the variable, so transplants change words more easily than practice.</li>
    <li>Convergence is the empirical result: precedent spread into civil systems, codification into common-law ones; the residue lives in procedure and style.</li>
    <li>Rule of law means law binding the state itself — a continuous gradient, not a binary, with episodic capture the intermediate case.</li>
    <li>Procedure is where citizens feel the family: adversarial fair fight versus judge-directed inquiry, each priced differently.</li>
    <li>Every system rations justice somewhere — the families just ration at different gates (costs, legal aid, waiting time).</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    Two sibling expert lessons continue the argument. Jurisprudence: what is law? takes the
    theoretical question this lesson kept comparative — what makes a rule law at all — and
    International law studies the legal order no single state can supply: law beyond the
    state. For the doctrinal foundations the comparisons assumed, the advanced lessons
    Contracts, Torts and liability and Property law are the natural revision loop.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Convergence, formally stated.</strong> The families borrow: constitutional review
    (Kelsen's Austrian model) spread from civil law everywhere; precedent practices
    strengthened in civil courts; common-law systems codified procedure and, increasingly,
    substantive fields. Mattei's taxonomy adds a third pole — the <em>rule of professional
    law</em> versus political law — arguing the real divide is between systems where law is
    autonomous and systems where it is an instrument. The empirical "legal origins" literature
    (La Porta and colleagues) claims family of origin predicts economic outcomes (common-law
    systems, stronger investor protection), and its contested findings generated a generation
    of econometric comparative law — with the serious critique that origin correlates with
    everything else the colonisers brought.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Open questions.</strong> Does global commerce make national families obsolete
    (CISG, Incoterms, international arbitration as a private transnational system — the
    "lex mercatoria" debate)? Can religious and state legal orders coexist within one polity
    (India's personal-law pluralism; religious arbitration in family matters) without
    corroding equal citizenship? And what becomes of the comparative method when AI translation
    makes every foreign statute instantly readable — does comparison become trivial, or does
    expertise migrate to the one thing that cannot be machine-read: why the rules differ?
  </p>

